# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored — never commit real secrets.
#
#   cp apps/api/.env.example apps/api/.env

NODE_ENV="development"
PORT="3002"

# --- Medallion database tiers (both required) ---
#
# Gold: this service's own store. Read-write. Owns its schema — apply it with
#   pnpm --filter api db:migrate
GOLD_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/gold
#
# Silver: the shared TimescaleDB machine-telemetry store. HOT, and READ-ONLY
# from this service: the connection opens with default_transaction_read_only, so
# any write fails. Its hypertables, continuous aggregates and compression /
# retention policies are owned by ../vitual-factory/docs/timescale-schema.sql
# and populated by Telegraf — do not migrate it from here.
SILVER_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@localhost:5433/silver
#
# Optional per-tier pool tuning. Defaults: MAX_CONN 20, IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS 30000,
# CONN_TIMEOUT_MS 5000. Each tier is tuned independently.
# GOLD_DATABASE_MAX_CONN=20
# GOLD_DATABASE_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# GOLD_DATABASE_CONN_TIMEOUT_MS=5000
# SILVER_DATABASE_MAX_CONN=20
# SILVER_DATABASE_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# SILVER_DATABASE_CONN_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# --- OIDC / Keycloak ---
OIDC_HOST=https://identity.example.test
OIDC_BASE_URI=https://identity.example.test/realms/your-realm
OIDC_TOKEN_URI=https://identity.example.test/realms/your-realm/protocol/openid-connect/token
OIDC_CLIENT_ID=your-client-id
OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=your-client-secret
KEYCLOAK_REALMS=your-realm
# Optional, default 30000.
# KEYCLOAK_TIMEOUT=30000

# --- Service identity ---
# REGION_CODE is the tenancy discriminator every Gold query filters on.
REGION_CODE=ap3
# APPLICATION_ALIAS also becomes the pg `application_name`, suffixed per tier
# (e.g. mes-oee-monitor-gold), so connections are identifiable in pg_stat_activity.
APPLICATION_ALIAS=mes-oee-monitor
# There is no plant time zone here. Shift boundaries are wall-clock times
# (1: 07:00-15:00, 2: 15:00-23:00, 3: 23:00-07:00 next day), so the downtime
# timeline does need a zone to turn a business date into an absolute window —
# but it resolves one per scope through mes-master-data (work unit -> work
# center -> area -> site.timezone) rather than reading a plant-wide constant.
# MASTER_DATA_BASE_URL below is therefore load-bearing for the timeline, not
# just for labels.

# Comma-separated. Defaults to http://localhost:3000 when unset.
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3001

# --- mes-master-data (upstream service) ---
# Area, work-center and work-unit records live in the mes-master-data service, not
# in either database tier here. The work-unit shift report calls it to resolve the
# workCenterId filter and to label rows, and /api/v1/master-data/* serves its area
# and work-center lists straight through to the browser.
#
# Required: no default. A plausible-but-wrong localhost default would surface as
# an opaque connection error at request time instead of failing at boot.
#
# NOTE the port: mes-master-data's api ALSO defaults to 3002, the same as PORT
# above. Running both locally means giving one of them a different port — pointing
# this at 3002 while this service listens on 3002 makes it call itself, and the
# symptom is a 404 from `/v1/areas` rather than anything that names the mistake.
MASTER_DATA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:4002
# Optional, default 5000. Per-request budget; the report makes up to 3 calls.
# MASTER_DATA_TIMEOUT_MS=5000
# Optional. Audience to REQUEST when minting the outbound service token — i.e.
# who we are calling. Distinct from AUTH_AUDIENCE below.
#
# It is documentation of intent more than a control: Keycloak ignores the
# `audience` form parameter, so what actually lands in `aud` comes from the
# audience protocol-mapper on OIDC_CLIENT_ID. Setting this to a value the mapper
# does not emit changes nothing — the callee still 401s.
# MASTER_DATA_AUDIENCE=mes-master-data
#
# NOTE: the outbound token reuses OIDC_CLIENT_ID / OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET above via
# the client_credentials grant, so that client needs service accounts enabled.
# It must be THIS service's own client — not the callee's.

# --- Inbound token checks (both opt-in, both OFF when unset) ---
# Leaving these unset reproduces the original behaviour exactly: signature,
# issuer and expiry only.
#
# ORDER MATTERS. Keycloak emits `aud: ["account"]` by default, so enabling
# AUTH_AUDIENCE before the realm has an audience protocol-mapper 401s EVERY
# caller, browser sessions included. Add the mapper, verify a real token carries
# the audience, and only then set this. Rollback is unsetting it.
# AUTH_AUDIENCE=mes-oee-monitor
#
# Comma-separated allowlist of `azp` (the client_id a token was issued to).
# Must name every legitimate caller — the browser-facing BFF client AND any
# service-account client — or enabling it locks out whichever is omitted.
# The browser-facing client is `mes-oee-monitor` (see apps/bff/.env.example),
# NOT `mes-oee-monitor-bff` — the realm has no client by that name.
# AUTH_ALLOWED_AZP=mes-oee-monitor,mes-oee-monitor-svc

# --- Operator mutation role gates (all three opt-in, all OFF when unset) ---
# TODOS.md §34. Unset means "any authenticated caller may do this", which is the
# behaviour every deployment has had so far. `closed_by` / `changed_by` record
# WHO regardless; these decide WHETHER.
#
# READ AS REALM ROLES, from `realm_access.roles`. Client roles under
# `resource_access` are NOT checked, and the reason matters: the only client id
# this service holds is OIDC_CLIENT_ID above, which is its own service account —
# an operator's browser token is issued to the BFF's client, so keying on it
# would miss on every real request. If your realm defines these as client roles,
# that needs a new variable naming the browser-facing client; ask before setting
# anything here.
#
# THE TRAP IS SHARPER THAN AUTH_AUDIENCE's. A wrong audience 401s everyone, which
# looks like an auth fault and gets diagnosed as one. A role name Keycloak does
# not emit 403s only these three write paths — every read keeps working and every
# page renders, so it stays invisible until somebody tries to sign off a shift at
# the end of one.
#
# SO ROLL OUT ONE AT A TIME: set one, have a real operator exercise that one
# route, and watch `operator_authorization_denials_total{action=...}` before
# setting the next. Rollback is unsetting it.
#
# THREE VARIABLES, NOT ONE, deliberately. Approving a shift report is a sign-off;
# reopening one UNDOES somebody else's sign-off; editing a downtime is routine
# data correction. Granting all three together is a choice you can make by
# setting them to the same role — the code will not make it for you.
# APPROVE_ROLE=oee-approver
# REOPEN_ROLE=oee-supervisor
# EDIT_DOWNTIME_ROLE=oee-downtime-editor
